WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.507
Kaitlyn was practicing her pitching at the baseball field.

2
00:00:06.508 --> 00:00:09.739
She reached into her bucket for another ball.

3
00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:13.990
Suddenly— beep, beep,

4
00:00:13.991 --> 00:00:17.537
beep! A big truck backed slowly onto the

5
00:00:17.538 --> 00:00:21.883
field. "Hey!" Kaitlyn thought. "What's that doing

6
00:00:21.884 --> 00:00:27.624
here?" A man got out of the truck. "Sorry," he

7
00:00:27.625 --> 00:00:31.515
said. "We need to set up here for tonight's fireworks

8
00:00:31.516 --> 00:00:34.411
display. You'll have to move somewhere

9
00:00:34.412 --> 00:00:39.317
else." "The Fourth of July show is still hours away," Kaitlyn

10
00:00:39.318 --> 00:00:42.485
said. "Can't I practice my pitching some

11
00:00:42.486 --> 00:00:45.595
more?" "I'm afraid not," the man

12
00:00:45.596 --> 00:00:49.624
said. "It takes several hours to set up a fireworks

13
00:00:49.625 --> 00:00:52.620
display." By now more men had

14
00:00:52.621 --> 00:00:56.289
arrived. They were moving wooden racks and boxes onto the

15
00:00:56.290 --> 00:01:01.874
field. Kaitlyn sighed. She looked closely at a

16
00:01:01.875 --> 00:01:07.204
rack. It held short plastic pipes. "Those are

17
00:01:07.205 --> 00:01:09.325
fireworks?" "Nope," the man

18
00:01:09.326 --> 00:01:13.440
said. "Those are cannons that shoot the fireworks into the

19
00:01:13.441 --> 00:01:17.825
sky." He opened a box and took out a brown object wrapped in

20
00:01:17.826 --> 00:01:22.079
plastic. It was about the size of a baseball and looked like a little

21
00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.977
bomb. "This is a firework

22
00:01:24.978 --> 00:01:28.224
shell." "What's inside of it?" Kaitlyn

23
00:01:28.225 --> 00:01:30.817
asked. "I'll explain," said the

24
00:01:30.818 --> 00:01:34.184
man. He set the shell gently back into the

25
00:01:34.185 --> 00:01:38.562
box. Then he bent down and drew a big circle in the

26
00:01:38.563 --> 00:01:43.866
dirt. "A shell is filled with little balls of

27
00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:48.673
gunpowder. Pyrotechnicians, like me, call these balls

28
00:01:48.674 --> 00:01:53.887
'stars.'" He picked up some baseballs and formed a pattern inside the circle with

29
00:01:53.888 --> 00:01:58.171
them. "The pattern of the stars gives a firework its

30
00:01:58.172 --> 00:02:02.295
shape." "What gives a firework its color?" Kaitlyn asked.

31
00:02:02.296 --> 00:02:06.642
"Magic!" The man laughed.

32
00:02:06.643 --> 00:02:10.997
Kaitlyn didn't. She really wanted to know the answer!

33
00:02:10.998 --> 00:02:15.415
"Well, the magic of science," the man added quickly.

34
00:02:15.416 --> 00:02:20.549
"Pyrotechnicians add chemicals to the gunpowder stars.

35
00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:24.340
When these chemicals burn, they make different colors.

36
00:02:24.341 --> 00:02:30.634
Like chemicals containing copper, for example—can you guess what color they make when they burn?"

37
00:02:30.635 --> 00:02:36.592
Kaitlyn pulled a penny from her pocket and glanced at it. "Orangish brown?"

38
00:02:36.593 --> 00:02:38.555
"Nope!" the man said.

39
00:02:38.556 --> 00:02:41.242
"Chemicals with copper make blue.

40
00:02:41.243 --> 00:02:43.982
Chemicals with calcium make orange."

41
00:02:43.983 --> 00:02:47.965
Next the man drew a square attached to the circle.

42
00:02:47.966 --> 00:02:52.252
"There's even more gunpowder here at the bottom of the shell."

43
00:02:52.253 --> 00:02:55.649
He drew a long line from the center of the circle.

44
00:02:55.650 --> 00:03:00.127
"This is the fuse. It's a string that lights the shell.

45
00:03:00.128 --> 00:03:06.014
When the shell is in the cannon, the fuse lights the gunpowder at the bottom of the shell.

46
00:03:06.015 --> 00:03:10.880
That explosion pushes the shell out of the cannon and into the air."

47
00:03:10.881 --> 00:03:13.935
"The shell doesn't burst inside the cannon?"

48
00:03:13.936 --> 00:03:17.978
"Nope. The fuse continues to burn in the air.

49
00:03:17.979 --> 00:03:22.340
Finally it explodes the shell and lights the stars.

50
00:03:22.341 --> 00:03:26.027
That's when you see the firework in the sky."

51
00:03:26.028 --> 00:03:28.876
"Awesome," Kaitlyn said.

52
00:03:28.877 --> 00:03:33.453
"Hmm. I guess you do have a lot to do before tonight."

53
00:03:33.454 --> 00:03:37.491
"Right. It's a big show with many fireworks!

54
00:03:37.492 --> 00:03:40.721
And we have to work carefully and safely.

55
00:03:40.722 --> 00:03:44.169
Working with fireworks is very dangerous."

56
00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:47.240
The man helped Kaitlyn gather her baseballs.

57
00:03:47.241 --> 00:03:53.295
"Are you going to watch the display tonight?" he asked, dropping the last baseball into her bucket.

58
00:03:53.296 --> 00:04:05.734
Kaitlyn smiled. "I can't wait!"

